\f1\b0 \cf0 BootUnlock unlocks the encrypted APFS volumes you choose when the Mac starts, before anybody logs in.\
\
\f0\b What is installed\
\f1\b0 The package puts its files into the
\f2  /Library/PrivilegedHelperTools/au.com.openwall.BootUnlock
\f1  directory: a few shell scripts, an XSL file, three small dialog scripts and a keychain helper program.  It adds a launch daemon in
\f2  /Library/LaunchDaemons
\f1 , a log rotation rule in
\f2  /etc/newsyslog.d
\f1 , and it writes a log to
\f2  /var/log/BootUnlock.log
\f1 .\
\
\f0\b How it works\
\f1\b0 The launch daemon runs the helper script at startup and whenever a volume is mounted.  For every volume you enrolled that is locked, the script takes the passphrase from the System keychain and unlocks the volume.  It leaves all other volumes alone, and it stops trying a volume after three failed attempts until you enrol it again.\
\
\f0\b Enrolling volumes\
\f1\b0 A window asking which volumes to unlock appears when the installation is finished.  For every volume you select you are asked for its passphrase, which is checked against the volume and then stored in the System keychain.  To change your choice later, and after every upgrade of BootUnlock, run
\f2  /Library/PrivilegedHelperTools/au.com.openwall.BootUnlock/update.sh
\f1 .\
\
\f0\b What you should know\
\f1\b0 The stored passphrases can only be read through the installed keychain helper, and that program only works for the root user.  Anybody who is root on this Mac, or who can read an unencrypted startup disk, can therefore get them: turn on FileVault for the startup disk, otherwise the encryption of the enrolled volumes protects very little.  Enrolled volumes are unlocked for everybody who uses this Mac, as soon as it has started.\
\
\f0\b Removing BootUnlock\
\f1\b0 Run
\f2  /Library/PrivilegedHelperTools/au.com.openwall.BootUnlock/uninstall.sh
\f1 .  It removes everything listed above, including the stored passphrases.}
